<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vicente Guerrero</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1782">1782</a>–<a href="/years/1831">1831</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vicente <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrero">Guerrero</a> was a leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-war-of-independence">Mexican War of Independence</a> who kept up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> resistance in the south after 1815. In 1821 he joined the royalist officer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agustin-de-iturbide">Agustín de Iturbide</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plan-of-iguala">Plan of Iguala</a>, which brought independence. As president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> in 1829 he abolished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, but he was overthrown that year and executed by the conservative government in 1831.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vicente-guerrero.jpg" alt="Vicente Guerrero" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anacleto Escutia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vicente_Guerrero.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Vicente <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrero">Guerrero</a> was born in Tixtla, a town in the Sierra Madre del Sur about 100 kilometres inland from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acapulco">Acapulco</a>. He worked in his father's mule-driven freight business, which took him across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> and exposed him to ideas of independence. He joined the insurgent army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-maria-morelos">José María Morelos</a> in late 1810. Because he spoke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a>, he was able to recruit support among Indigenous communities, and in a speech at Tlapa in 1815 he argued for citizenship to be extended to Indigenous people.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morelos">Morelos</a> was captured and executed in December 1815, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrero">Guerrero</a> became one of the last major insurgent commanders in the field. From 1816 to 1821 he kept the war alive by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> warfare in the Tierra Caliente, refusing viceregal offers of pardon even when his father carried an appeal for him to surrender.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>In 1821 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrero">Guerrero</a> held the royalist commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agustin-de-iturbide">Agustín de Iturbide</a> to a stalemate and appealed to him to join the cause of independence. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plan-of-iguala">Plan of Iguala</a> of 24 February 1821 proclaimed independence and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a>, kept the place of the Catholic Church and made all inhabitants citizens without distinction of European, African or Indian origins. Their combined forces entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> on 27 September 1821. In 1823 Guerrero joined the rising against Emperor Iturbide, and he was afterwards named one of the ruling triumvirate.</p><p>The September 1828 election, decided by the state legislatures, went to Manuel Gómez Pedraza, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrero">Guerrero</a> second. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-lopez-de-santa-anna">Antonio López de Santa Anna</a> rose in support of Guerrero, fighting broke out in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>, and Gómez Pedraza resigned and went into exile. Guerrero took office on 1 April 1829 with the conservative <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anastasio-bustamante">Anastasio Bustamante</a> as vice president. Facing an empty treasury and a Spanish attempt at reconquest, he used extraordinary powers to abolish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> across almost all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> in September 1829, though he exempted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> on 2 December 1829 under pressure from settlers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Bustamante's rebellion began on 4 December 1829, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrero">Guerrero</a> was deposed on 17 December. He withdrew to the south and fought through 1830. In January 1831 the Italian captain Francisco Picaluga, who was paid 50,000 pesos by the government, invited him aboard his ship at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acapulco">Acapulco</a> and handed him to federal troops. After a court-martial he was shot at Cuilapam on 14 February 1831, an act the liberal press called judicial murder.</p><p>His remains were returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> in 1842. The state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrero">Guerrero</a> is named for him, and his reported reply to his father, La Patria es Primero, is written in gold inside the Chamber of Deputies.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vicente Guerrero” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Royalist armies then contained the insurgency, which survived as guerrilla warfare under <b>Vicente Guerrero</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-spain">New Spain · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Agustín de Iturbide and <b>Vicente Guerrero</b> proclaimed a plan for an independent Mexican monarchy guaranteeing Catholicism and equality.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-24">24 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In February 1821 the royalist officer Agustín de Iturbide joined the insurgent <b>Vicente Guerrero</b> in the Plan of Iguala, promising independence, Catholicism and equality under a constitutional monarchy.</blockquote><a href="/state/first-mexican-empire">First Mexican Empire</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Vicente Guerrero</b> abolished slavery in 1829, the year Santa Anna defeated a Spanish reconquest attempt at Tampico.</blockquote><a href="/state/first-mexican-republic">First Mexican Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Tixtla de Guerrero, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. Died in Cuilápam de Guerrero, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=17.57,-99.4&amp;year=1813" aria-label="Tixtla de Guerrero on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="1851 1512 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Vicente Guerrero"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2301 1782h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2301 1782h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=17.57,-99.4&amp;year=1813">What was here in 1813? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
